  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 60af0432a3c869e48a591344a8dafd71, type: 3}
  m_Name: PatPre_1
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  question: "During a conversation about your upcoming procedure, your doctor explains
    that there may be a small amount of leftover blood or tissue not needed for your
    care. Normally it would be discarded, but you\u2019re asked if you\u2019d like
    to donate it to a research biobank instead. While your doctor steps out, you
    quickly Google and read, \u201CPatient samples, such as blood or tissue, can
    be used in preclinical research to investigate how a treatment might work.\u201D"
  explanation: Patient samples help researchers study how a disease works, identify
    potential targets, and test effects on human cells or tissues. Although preclinical
    research mainly involves lab (in vitro) and animal (in vivo) studies, patient
    samples provide human-relevant information that complements these studies, helping
    scientists better predict safety and potential effectiveness before treatments
    enter clinical trials.
  correctAnswer: True
  incorrectAnswer1: False
  incorrectAnswer2: 
  incorrectAnswer3: 
  questionRole: Patient
  questionType: TrueFalse
  questionStage: Preclinical
